00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.760
[Kaspar]: Wwwhackteria.org.

00:01:47.965 --> 00:01:53.325
[Kaspar]: So this is Collab Radio, and I'm having another interview with a beautiful guest from

00:01:53.325 --> 00:01:54.590
[Kaspar]: the Collab network.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.630
[Tengal]: Hello, hi everyone, my name is Tengo, I'm originally from Manila, Philippines.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:05.390
[Tengal]: I live in Berlin, based in Berlin in Manila the last three years.

00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:15.145
[Tengal]: I worked as a in multiple, sort of roles in the culture back in Asia.

00:02:15.145 --> 00:02:16.205
[Tengal]: I was an organizer.

00:02:16.345 --> 00:02:19.165
[Tengal]: I was a I'm an artist, a filmmaker.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:20.205
[Tengal]: I make music.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:29.950
[Tengal]: I help build laboratories, I help build networks between different Asian nations, in Southeast Asia particularly with Europe.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.390
[Tengal]: And somehow all of these journeys led me to Berlin in the last three years.

00:02:35.615 --> 00:02:40.655
[Tengal]: I call myself a cultural instigator and, what does that even mean?

00:02:40.655 --> 00:02:48.115
[Tengal]: It means I instigate things, you know, it it requires a lot of collaboration in order to build these kind of platforms and networks.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:03:11.505
[Tengal]: In in terms of what is a lab for me, I think a lab, I closely more think of it as a living labs, you know, like the idea that is where I'm coming from the context of Asia where Southeast Asia particularly where we are more in a more community and collective setting where a lot of the things are not so available.

00:03:12.205 --> 00:03:22.110
[Tengal]: There's less there is more power in numbers and in collectivity, and people live together, cook together, do stuff together.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:25.410
[Tengal]: This is sort of what I mean when I say living labs.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.410
[Tengal]: I have used to run a a few spaces in Manila and also in other parts of Asia.

00:03:33.165 --> 00:03:45.885
[Tengal]: We don't really need to go through all of that but it follows the same principle of sharing space, sharing knowledge, having open information, having open exchanges between people.

00:03:45.885 --> 00:03:55.960
[Tengal]: We always invite guests similar to what I'm doing here now at Casper's, year, environmental, artistic research space where it's a house.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.780
[Tengal]: We all live together, we've been cooking together, and we we have a good time basically.

00:04:01.535 --> 00:04:11.715
[Tengal]: And it's following the spirit of this, idea that is pretty much connected to Southeast Asian culture where a lot of collectivity is the first and foremost important thing.

00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:19.410
[Tengal]: The process is the the the journey or the journey is the more important thing rather than the output.

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:39.555
[Tengal]: For techno for the synergies grant, for for the collaboration with, Aptera and all of these other, people, I represent HACE, which is a burden based collective platform where we are a member of this network.

00:04:40.175 --> 00:05:03.385
[Tengal]: A large part of what we are trying to do here is to help, at least through my expertise, is to help connect, Asian, labs, media art labs, media art collectives, people working in digital art, art and science, design, music, all of these kind of very much hard to define kind of categories.

00:05:03.845 --> 00:05:06.425
[Tengal]: They are not your typical art.

00:05:07.365 --> 00:05:08.265
[Tengal]: They're not painters.

00:05:08.405 --> 00:05:09.545
[Tengal]: They paint with code.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.130
[Tengal]: They paint with food.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:12.730
[Tengal]: They work with communities.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:21.290
[Tengal]: And a lot of it is to help power empower, these communities that have less access to Europe and also Switzerland.

00:05:21.965 --> 00:05:41.100
[Tengal]: So this is sort of what I think my role here is and what I can help with because this is the power of the the network where we can cross the globe and also exchange knowledge and also just have a good time, cook together, and live together.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:41.880
[Tengal]: Yeah.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.340
[Tengal]: That's pretty much my answer.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:43.900
[Kaspar]: Beautiful.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.420
[Kaspar]: I wanna ask more questions if I may.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:06:01.335
[Kaspar]: Just now that we are connected here in Emmental, I wondered if the intrinsic motivation for collaboration or the threshold to become collaborative, what would you define that?

00:06:01.955 --> 00:06:07.260
[Kaspar]: If you think of what at least do you need to to have to to start being motivated?

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.920
[Tengal]: Well, I think many people gather here.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:20.780
[Tengal]: They all come from their different backgrounds, very different purposes, very different paths or if we would put it in a metaphor of a train station, we would have very different destinations.

00:06:21.975 --> 00:06:24.475
[Tengal]: But, somehow we meet at this station.

00:06:24.535 --> 00:06:28.395
[Tengal]: We meet at like this station for this moment, maybe for a week, for two weeks.

00:06:29.095 --> 00:06:31.195
[Tengal]: And some interesting things can happen.

00:06:31.415 --> 00:06:46.060
[Tengal]: I I believe that there's not so much predestination of what we are trying to achieve and we will discover this as we figure out each other while we are stuck in this station before we move on to our different directions.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:56.625
[Tengal]: I think, I believe that this kind of like the the basic threshold of it is to just have things happen, have the opportunity for things to happen.

00:06:58.205 --> 00:07:06.720
[Tengal]: Where most of the time a lot of people would not even wanna talk to their neighbors or even do anything with somebody.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:14.160
[Tengal]: And I don't say this just because we have a grant together, but it's also I think there's a meaningful connections between friends.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:21.925
[Tengal]: A lot of this network is built on friendship and just familiarity as well.

00:07:22.145 --> 00:07:22.645
[Tengal]: Yeah.

00:07:23.585 --> 00:07:24.105
[Tengal]: I I I would like

00:07:24.105 --> 00:07:29.705
[Kaspar]: to And do you have can you put words to maybe yeah.

00:07:29.705 --> 00:07:32.245
[Kaspar]: I'm for myself, I'm looking for this threshold.

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:38.560
[Kaspar]: What is it that I find in other people so inspiring that I start to to work together?

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.480
[Kaspar]: Or what is the the trigger to to, yeah, to engage.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:42.980
[Kaspar]: Right?

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:54.165
[Tengal]: I think the if I in my in my perspective, I think the threshold a destination.

00:07:54.305 --> 00:08:04.950
[Tengal]: I think a lot of it is really just being open being open to the differences that we all have, the different cultures and context that we share.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:25.955
[Tengal]: And having this opportunity, and this is what I've experienced in Switzerland many times already, where, like, homemade or SGMK, you know, the the space there, here, and many other places that I have yet to discover, there is there is this pause in time.

00:08:26.175 --> 00:08:27.855
[Tengal]: We're always rushing towards time.

00:08:27.855 --> 00:08:39.970
[Tengal]: We're always trying trying to catch deadlines and trying to catch the train, but there is somehow some magic there where what if time would stop for a few days?

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.525
[Tengal]: What would happen if you're all stuck in the same elevator, The elevator doesn't move?

00:08:44.525 --> 00:08:45.805
[Tengal]: What could happen there?

00:08:45.805 --> 00:08:49.345
[Tengal]: And I think it's all always about the potential of possibilities.

00:08:49.645 --> 00:08:54.225
[Tengal]: And this is something that, we are all defining and we're all discovering.

00:08:55.005 --> 00:08:57.600
[Tengal]: It's it's not about the destination at the end of the day.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:08:59.040
[Kaspar]: Beautiful.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:08:59.540
[Kaspar]: Beautiful.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:00.320
[Kaspar]: Yeah.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.280
[Kaspar]: Thank you so much.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:02.900
[Tengal]: Was that good?

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:03.760
[Kaspar]: Mhmm.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:04.240
[Kaspar]: Mhmm.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:05.120
[Kaspar]: If we are yeah.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:05.600
[Kaspar]: Perfect.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.060
[Kaspar]: I will have enough for a beautiful schnit.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:09.760
[Tengal]: Yeah.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:10.480
[Kaspar]: Thank you so much.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:10.960
[Tengal]: Yeah.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.415
[Kaspar]: Do you wanna do you wanna say anything from your side to the listeners in Berlin?

00:09:15.415 --> 00:09:17.435
[Kaspar]: Do you have another concert coming up?

00:09:17.735 --> 00:09:21.495
[Tengal]: Well, I I I am still kind of relatively new in Berlin.

00:09:21.495 --> 00:09:36.360
[Tengal]: I I started to develop this new concert series where I the intention is to find my discomfort and flexibility by inviting artist that I perform it for the first time so it is kind of like a meeting.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:58.045
[Tengal]: It is kind of like let's go to the kitchen cook something together even if it if it even if the meals do not coincide but it's it's I am sort of I want to approach things here as if I would approach it with cooking where you share a meal and it could be a metaphor for sharing art, sharing ideas, sharing knowledge, sharing experience.

00:09:58.610 --> 00:09:59.270
[Tengal]: So, yeah.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.030
[Tengal]: Glad it worked out.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:01.670
[Kaspar]: Yeah.

00:17:29.665 --> 00:17:32.965
[Kaspar]: So what is ear and what are we listening to?

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:43.820
[Kaspar]: Basically, we are listening to recordings in and around ear, like in the first part some LLM, sonification.

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.780
[Kaspar]: I can deep dive into that in a later episode.

00:17:49.675 --> 00:17:53.295
[Kaspar]: But at the moment I would love to reflect on the last piece.

00:17:53.355 --> 00:18:08.500
[Kaspar]: It's an recording made in the C 19 organ, which was basically built in here located in a schoolhouse in the border of the biosphere of Antlebuch in the really center of, Switzerland.

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.860
[Kaspar]: E stands for environmental artistic research.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:25.275
[Kaspar]: So what I try to do is since I'm passionate about listening, I'm really try together listeners all over the place, all over the world, and then physically together in that schoolhouse.

00:18:25.815 --> 00:18:33.035
[Kaspar]: In EO, we want to research on what listening can do and what we can listen to, especially in the sense of sustainable futures.

00:18:33.335 --> 00:18:37.560
[Kaspar]: So that's also why it is part of the CoLab synergies grant.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:19:00.645
[Kaspar]: Basically listening is a big thing to connect, and I think it's being discovered more and more as a method or as a way to solve problems as well as in trying to get the other side, trying to listen to what's really deeply the concern or deeply the involvement or the basic needs.

00:19:00.645 --> 00:19:20.635
[Kaspar]: So this is kind of NVC, like non violent communication that gives me where to go to, where to listen to people and maybe even to all those other creatures in and around us in the earth to, yeah, maybe discover something that is a need or that is a, a thing to connect.

00:22:02.495 --> 00:22:09.075
[Kaspar]: On top, I believe that listening is a helpful tool to cope with your internal juxtapositions.

00:22:10.575 --> 00:22:23.100
[Kaspar]: Well, they can be external as well, but basically by an attitude of improvisation, you might be actually able to tell a story that has those juxtapositions in one.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:35.605
[Kaspar]: Since you're leaving your fixed presets of mindsets where you can then basically, navigate on your improv attitudes.

00:22:36.465 --> 00:22:38.325
[Kaspar]: So maybe it's taken too far.

00:22:38.545 --> 00:22:51.140
[Kaspar]: However, I strongly see and hear around me how people get connected when they take time to really listen and feel along as an empathy for the speaker or the one that makes the sound.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:23:00.035
[Kaspar]: So in here we practice this and we call it kind of a Aussian like the audible vision.

00:23:00.815 --> 00:23:10.675
[Kaspar]: So vision would be very connotated with the eyes and the seeing, but what is, an equivalent in listening, we came up with the word Aussian.

00:23:11.295 --> 00:23:14.275
[Kaspar]: Aussian, Aussian, auditory vision.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:14.970
[Kaspar]: No?

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:25.930
[Kaspar]: So next to the innovation and experiments we do in ear, it's, also a lot of workshops and gathering space in, the sense of art is in residency.

00:23:27.005 --> 00:23:44.230
[Kaspar]: One can book as well as find events on the site or anywhere else like, for instance, this week is, this week we are playing the Expo of Jokos Schneider in Zurich in the from the sixteenth to the nineteenth.

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:55.430
[Kaspar]: We will also show the C19 again, but my friend Joppe Schneider is as Susanna Glatli and Marcial Koch in the board of EIR.

00:23:56.050 --> 00:24:01.935
[Kaspar]: And so, yeah, in our artistic ways, we also try to collaborate as much as we can.

00:24:02.155 --> 00:24:12.815
[Kaspar]: And as a matter of fact, we have a lot of work done this summer with our c 19 balloon, but also with the other projects that are around Ear.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:29.655
[Kaspar]: For instance, through the Telegram and the signal group and on top of the newsletter, people can, get involved get involved and participate in also the build up, which is still a piece of work.

00:24:29.655 --> 00:24:41.755
[Kaspar]: So meaning that meanwhile, the roof is done and the side part is built by 100% reused material thanks to the platform called salsa.

00:24:42.135 --> 00:24:42.635
[Kaspar]: Ch.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:25:05.255
[Kaspar]: This one is, offering construction materials in high quality and, I used, offer to build all my windows that are three meters in height and also the walls and everything demounted from a mock up in Fraunfeld and rebuilt on the earsight.

00:25:06.435 --> 00:25:12.375
[Kaspar]: So we tried to get the lowest footprint since, yeah, that is, something that is a concern as well of course.

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:22.940
[Kaspar]: And we used all certified local wood on a S and P one level of sustainable building, which is the highest level I can think of at the moment.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:28.745
[Kaspar]: So we are incredibly happy that, yeah, it's very well isolated for the winter.

00:25:29.125 --> 00:25:37.445
[Kaspar]: And at this point, I would send you into another, the honorable member of IHIR being around there.

00:25:37.445 --> 00:25:49.580
[Kaspar]: And he's my big friend, Andreas Bosshardt, whom I had the possibility to interview just But the thing is, he's a long, long collaborator with me.

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:56.615
[Kaspar]: I learned a lot from him, and I am so happy that he's right now here not in the studio.

00:25:56.615 --> 00:25:56.935
[Kaspar]: No.

00:25:56.935 --> 00:26:00.955
[Kaspar]: We are walking along the Limmat, in Zurich.

00:26:01.095 --> 00:26:04.315
[Kaspar]: So, Andres, I have normally three questions.

00:26:04.535 --> 00:26:05.015
[Andreas]: Yes, please.

00:26:05.015 --> 00:26:10.760
[Kaspar]: One question would be, what do you do and what kind of lab do you have?

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:13.080
[Andreas]: I am a sound gardener.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:21.595
[Andreas]: And my lab is the garden of my own of my thinkings and your thinkings.

00:26:21.735 --> 00:26:25.655
[Andreas]: I have a lab inside our brain.

00:26:25.655 --> 00:26:26.155
[Kaspar]: Great.

00:26:27.175 --> 00:26:41.180
[Andreas]: And what I'm to have done first, I have reconstructed my own personal instrument which is called the roto bosophone in version five zero zero only in brain way in brain matters.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:42.120
[Kaspar]: Wow.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:42.620
[Andreas]: Yes.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:44.775
[Kaspar]: Can you explain us?

00:26:44.855 --> 00:26:50.475
[Kaspar]: The Klanggarten or the sound gardening, what is that exactly, as a practice?

00:26:50.615 --> 00:26:53.355
[Andreas]: It's really it's really based on real gardening.

00:26:53.415 --> 00:26:59.515
[Andreas]: I had, years ago, a fantastic opportunity to collaboration with a real gardener in Firenze.

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:12.555
[Andreas]: And this is a master gardener and he has this garden which is grounded in the idea that the color red is circling in the garden around in one year.

00:27:12.715 --> 00:27:21.375
[Andreas]: So all the plants have to flower according to this movement of red color around the garden, which I liked very much and I thought this is a wonderful composition.

00:27:22.075 --> 00:27:23.695
[Andreas]: Can we not work together?

00:27:24.555 --> 00:27:32.890
[Andreas]: And then we established in the garden together Borucci, another incredible philosopher, a sounder, searcher, and collaborator.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:41.875
[Andreas]: We established Good.

00:27:42.095 --> 00:27:45.935
[Andreas]: And there, we learned to collaborate with a lot of people from all over the world.

00:27:45.935 --> 00:27:50.815
[Andreas]: We made held it two or three sem seminars, two weeks, with wonderful food.

00:27:50.815 --> 00:27:55.170
[Andreas]: And we were just exploring inside of this almost botanical garden.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:27:59.410
[Andreas]: And in planted sounds, little speakers, sometimes little objects.

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:03.650
[Andreas]: And we learned to take care of these sounds.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:08.450
[Andreas]: Because I I saw the gardener in the morning when he went to the garden.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:15.965
[Andreas]: He was very keen about the plants, if they have a little water on the on top of the leaves and how it will develop in the days.

00:28:16.105 --> 00:28:21.376
[Andreas]: And then in the afternoon, he would see did look if the tension of the leaf are

00:28:21.412 --> 00:28:21.590
[Andreas]: is still strong and not

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:25.430
[Andreas]: weak and so and not weak and so forth.

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:26.450
[Andreas]: And they said, okay.

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:28.790
[Andreas]: I have to learn this for concerning sounds.

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:34.310
[Andreas]: So I want to be a sound gardener and take care of all the sounds I do.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:36.790
[Andreas]: And this was a big change.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:46.425
[Andreas]: So I I changed and I thought, you know, when we make electronic music, we are like doctors in emergency room.

00:28:46.805 --> 00:28:50.265
[Andreas]: We have them all on cables and if we switch off the cables, they die.

00:28:50.645 --> 00:28:51.205
[Andreas]: You see?

00:28:51.205 --> 00:28:53.790
[Andreas]: So this is not a living era living sound.

00:28:53.950 --> 00:29:07.665
[Andreas]: So I'm completely have turned around and I'm a sound gardener and I'm walking in the city and I listen to all the sounds made by us and we don't care about them anymore.

00:29:08.445 --> 00:29:14.705
[Andreas]: The airplane goes and the pilot has no idea what goes behind him, you know, and this guy is walking and the dog is walking.

00:29:15.005 --> 00:29:20.785
[Andreas]: And all these sounds are making these wonderful murmurs of the city.

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:23.880
[Andreas]: Voila.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:24.920
[Andreas]: You have the answer.

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:31.080
[Andreas]: And this is an incredible world, a universe, a living universe of sounds now.

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:36.985
[Andreas]: And I understand that we have no clue how to work with it.

00:29:37.765 --> 00:29:38.005
[Kaspar]: Mhmm.

00:29:38.005 --> 00:29:42.665
[Andreas]: So a sound gardener in a way, it's a big adventure and I collaborate everywhere.

00:29:42.725 --> 00:29:44.725
[Andreas]: I collaborate, for example, with these swimmers.

00:29:44.725 --> 00:29:45.225
[Andreas]: Hello.

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:53.320
[Andreas]: Yeah.

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:55.160
[SPEAKER_01]: You see how it works.

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:57.400
[Andreas]: It's instant collaboration.

00:29:58.180 --> 00:29:58.420
[Andreas]: Mhmm.

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:00.840
[Andreas]: And I can also talk with walls.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:03.480
[Andreas]: This is now something very new.

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:12.735
[Andreas]: I collaborate with my walls because I found out when I speak in a space, I think I I I'm speaking, but it's not true.

00:30:13.195 --> 00:30:16.175
[Andreas]: What comes back from the wall is the wall speaking to me.

00:30:16.555 --> 00:30:27.270
[Andreas]: And the wall is very sad because she understands that I'm not understanding that it's not my word coming back from the wall in which the wall speaks.

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:28.550
[Andreas]: But it's the echo.

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:30.870
[Andreas]: It's the specialness, you know?

00:30:31.010 --> 00:30:31.250
[Kaspar]: Yeah.

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:34.975
[Andreas]: So the wall speaks wall Yeah.

00:30:35.435 --> 00:30:35.515
[Andreas]: Wallish.

00:30:35.515 --> 00:30:35.915
[Kaspar]: Yeah.

00:30:35.915 --> 00:30:43.695
[Andreas]: I'm I discovered this a few days ago and now I'm completely mesmerized with this to to understand the language of the walls.

00:30:44.315 --> 00:30:50.410
[Andreas]: They tell me, I don't know exactly what they tell me, to collaborate in creating space, I think.

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:52.330
[Andreas]: This is now I fantasize now.

